"""
Inverse-Transform Sampling — generate samples from any distribution via inverse CDF.

WHAT IT DOES: The universal trick: take a uniform u in (0,1), run it through the
distribution's inverse CDF, and out comes a sample from that distribution. This is
how random.expovariate and friends work under the hood — turn plain uniform randomness
into exponential, or any custom shape. Demonstrates both exponential and a custom
piecewise distribution, with empirical checks against known moments.

WHEN TO USE: You need samples from a distribution with a known, tractable inverse
CDF — exponential, Cauchy, logistic, or a custom piecewise distribution. When the
inverse CDF is closed-form, this is the most efficient and exact sampling method.

GUARANTEES: For exponential(rate): sample mean within 0.05 of 1/rate, sample variance
within 0.05 of 1/rate², all samples non-negative, median within 0.05 of ln(2)/rate.
For the piecewise distribution: support in [0,2], ~2/3 of samples in [0,1], empirical
CDF at x=1 within 0.05 of 2/3, mean within 0.1 of 5/6.

NOT: A general sampling library. Only works when the inverse CDF is closed-form and
tractable. The `rate` parameter is UNGUARDED — negative rate silently returns
impossible negative waiting times (fix #13).
"""
